1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:04,920
Since the 1930s, drivers here say their passenger vanished at a cemetery.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:07,400
Archer Avenue, south-west of Chicago.

3
00:00:07,670 --> 00:00:09,610
The stories start at a dance hall.

4
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:11,770
And end at Resurrection Cemetery.

5
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:16,070
In 1939, Jerry Palus said he met a girl named Mary.

6
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:17,780
He offered her a ride home.

7
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:19,940
By the cemetery, she asked to get out.

8
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:22,690
He said she crossed toward the gates, and was gone.

9
00:00:23,110 --> 00:00:27,090
In 1976, a driver reported a girl behind the gates.

10
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:28,380
Police found no one.

11
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:31,020
Just two bent bars, with marks like handprints.

12
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:34,010
The cemetery blamed a truck that backed into the fence.

13
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:35,910
And folklorists know this one:

14
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:38,170
the vanishing hitchhiker, told worldwide.

15
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:40,730
Several local girls have been named as Mary.

16
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,720
None confirmed.

17
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,180
So who was standing at the gates?

18
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:45,440
Nobody knows.

19
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:46,770
But on Archer Avenue,
